<section class="section alt" id="materials"><div class="wrap">
 <h2>Materials and pressure ratings</h2>
 <p>Choose the material for the fluid, then check the seat. It often sets the real pressure limit.</p>
 <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>A good choice for</th><th>Body CWP</th><th>With PTFE seats</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope='row'>316 stainless steel</th><td style='white-space:normal'>The default: water, air, gases, oils and most chemicals</td><td>6,000 psig (414 bar)</td><td>1,500 psig (103 bar)</td></tr><tr><th scope='row'>Brass</th><td style='white-space:normal'>Water, air and non-corrosive service at lower cost</td><td>3,000 psig (207 bar)</td><td>1,500 psig (103 bar)</td></tr><tr><th scope='row'>Monel alloy 400</th><td style='white-space:normal'>Seawater, brines and chloride-rich media</td><td>3,000 psig (207 bar)</td><td>1,500 psig (103 bar)</td></tr><tr><th scope='row'>Hastelloy C-276</th><td style='white-space:normal'>Aggressive acids and chemicals where stainless pits</td><td>4,000 psig (276 bar)</td><td>1,500 psig (103 bar)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p class="caption">Instrument series (01 to 04). Also available: 304/304L SS, carbon steel, Inconel and duplex.</p>
 <div class="note warn"><b>Seats set the limit.</b> A valve is only as strong as its weakest part. With PTFE seats these instrument valves are limited to 1,500 psig, even when the body is rated higher. Tell us your pressure, temperature and fluid and we'll confirm the right seat.</div>
</div></section>
