  <tr><th scope="row">Series 08</th><td style="white-space:normal">TE / MTE = inch / metric tube ends, MF = male NPT, FF = female NPT</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
</div></section>
<section class="section alt" id="care"><div class="wrap two">
 <div><h2>Installing</h2>
  <div class="note"><b>Check before you fit.</b> Confirm size, material and pressure rating on the valve marking against your specification. Flush the line of chips and debris first.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>Hold the body, not the handle.</b> Back up with a wrench on the body or end flats when tightening connections. Never use the handle as a lever.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>Threaded ends.</b> Apply sealant or tape to the male thread only, and keep it off the first thread so none enters the valve.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>Tube ends.</b> Cut tubing square, deburr, insert fully until it bottoms on the shoulder, and follow the tube fitting make-up procedure. Keep ferrule sets together.</div></div>
 <div><h2>Operating and care</h2>
  <div class="note"><b>Fully open or fully closed.</b> Ball valves are for on/off and switching duty. Running one part-open to throttle flow erodes the seats and causes leaks.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>Read the handle.</b> On two-way valves, handle in line with the pipe means open. On 3-way valves the handle points to the flow path.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>Stem weeping?</b> Snug the gland nut a small amount. The stem packing is adjustable with the valve still in line.</div>
  <div class="note warn"><b>Safety first.</b> Depressurize and drain the line before loosening any end connection or packing, and never exceed the rated pressure.</div></div>
</div></section>
